{
  "text": " Come on, keep walking 8400. I still need 1600 1600 watt steps 10,000 a day everybody knows that under that you're basically dying Everybody knows and who told everybody doctors scientists My watch your watch. Do you know who picked that number? No, not another word watch this Tokyo 1964 the Olympic Games turn a whole country toward fitness the next year a clock company wants its share of the fever. Its product, a little box that counts your steps. It needs a name. They call it \"Manpoké,\" the 10,000 step meter. Why 10,000? No study, no doctor, just a round number, and a symbol that looks like a man walking. The ads repeat it, walk 10,000 steps a day and the box sells. The slogan outlives the box. It spreads into gyms, magazines, health campaigns. Then come the fitness bands and smartwatches and 10,000 becomes the default goal on millions of risks. In 2019, a Harvard researcher looks for the study behind the number. There isn't one. The name was a marketing tool. In 2025, a review of 57 studies, most of the benefit arrives around 7,000 steps. And some who never reached 10,000 stop walking altogether. Walking is good for you. That part is true. But the number was never medicine. It was a slogan that sold a box. How many of your rules did someone write just to sell you something? You can keep counting someone else's numbers or go quiet. Watch, learn, like, subscribe and evolve quietly until you're the wolf.",
